\subsection*{1. Resonance Tensor Definition}

Let $\mathcal{C}_i$ and $\mathcal{C}_j$ be two Codex Capsules with
glyph-seals $\Gamma_i$ and $\Gamma_j$.  
We define the resonance tensor:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{ij}
= \Gamma_i \otimes \Gamma_j,
\label{eq:resonance_tensor}
\end{equation}
where $\otimes$ is the glyph-seal tensor product.

The resonance tensor encodes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item semantic coupling,
    \item lineage inheritance,
    \item dynamical compatibility,
    \item cryptographic continuity.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{2. Resonance Manifold}

We define the resonance manifold $\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{res}}$ as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{res}}
= \left\{
(\mathcal{C}_i, \mathcal{C}_j) \;\middle|\;
\mathcal{R}_{ij} \neq 0
\right\}.
\end{equation}

A pair of capsules lies on the resonance manifold if and only if their
glyph-seals produce a non-zero tensor product.

This manifold is the domain of valid capsule interactions.

\subsection*{3. Resonance Validity Condition}

Resonance is valid if:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{ij} \neq 0
\quad \Longrightarrow \quad
\mathcal{C}_i \prec \mathcal{C}_j,
\end{equation}
where $\prec$ is the lineage ordering relation.

Thus:
\begin{itemize}
    \item resonance implies lineage,
    \item lineage implies semantic inheritance,
    \item semantic inheritance implies stability constraints.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{4. Dynamical Stability of Resonance}

Capsule resonance must satisfy the Lyapunov stability condition:
